    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Adolescence is a period of emotional changes. Some teenagers have trouble dealing with these changes, resulting in disruptive behavior. These issues especially affect high-risk adolescents. Young people who grow up in disadvantaged, violent neighborhoods are constantly exposed to life threats, generating harmful psychological conditions which lead to the adoption of aggressive, short-term motivated behavior. Many times this leads to incarceration in juvenile centers and/or participation in special programs.\r\nTeaching students to understand their emotions and methods for dealing with them can give these students a chance to be happier, focus more and do better in school.\r\nTinker.me is a program that uses a project-based learning approach to teach students emotional awareness.  This is accomplished by teaching students the automatic reactions their emotions have on them, that these physical reactions can be measured and made visible, and that there are techniques to regulate these physical impacts. Using an electronic toolkit—a microcontroller and set of sensors, including physiological sensors—students learn to measure the impact emotional states have on them and then design and build their own personally-relevant emotional awareness device to demonstrate learnings.\r\n Early testing has shown that students are engaged with the emotional awareness content and the maker activities. We are confident that Tinker.me will improve students’ self-perception about emotional state, confidence and self-esteem as learners, knowledge about emotional responses and mindfulness techniques.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
